FIELD 1 — LITERALLY MEASURED
----------------------------

Instrumental target:
- Monthly gridded land-air and sea-surface temperature anomalies, 1902–1995.
- Calibration interval: 1902–1980.
- Principal verification interval: 1854–1901, withheld from calibration.
- Grid: 5° × 5°, with 1,082 nearly continuous grid-point series in the instrumental field.
- Derived large-scale quantity relevant to Field 6: areally weighted Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperature anomaly.

Proxy/instrumental predictor network:
- 112 indicators available back to 1820.
- 93 back to 1760.
- 74 back to 1700.
- 57 back to 1600.
- 24 back to 1450.
- 22 back to 1400.

Endpoint performance reported by the source:
- At AD 1400, the network resolves only the first eigenvector.
- Approximately 40–50% of Northern Hemisphere mean variance is reported as resolved in calibration and verification.
- The paper states that no useful spatial patterns are resolved that far back.
- The paper terminates its useful reconstruction at AD 1400 because still sparser earlier networks show little evidence of skill even for the first eigenvector.

Unit and comparison:
- Annual reconstructed anomaly.
- Comparison of three late instrumental-era years—1990, 1995, and an updated 1997 value—with all reconstructed annual values back to 1400.
- Geographic scope: Northern Hemisphere mean, not uniform global or hemispheric local warming.

FIELD 2 — SOURCE’S STRONGEST CLAIM
----------------------------------

Abstract formulation:
“Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperatures for three of the past eight years are warmer than any other year since (at least) AD 1400.”

Results formulation:
“Taking into account the uncertainties in our NH reconstruction … it appears that the years 1990, 1995 and now 1997 (this value recently calculated and not shown) each show anomalies that are greater than any other year back to 1400 at 3 standard errors, or roughly a 99.7% level of certainty.”

Immediate scope qualification:
“We note that hemispheric mean values are not associated with globally or hemispherically uniform trends.”

Difference in prominence:
- The abstract gives the comparative warmth claim without the standard-error wording.
- The results provide the stronger quantified confidence statement.
- Field 6 follows the prompt’s default rule and selects the quantified results claim.

FIELD 3 — WEAKER DEFENSIBLE CLAIM
---------------------------------

The evidence-matched formulation does not remove the finding. It binds it to:
- the MBH98 proxy network;
- the MBH98 calibration and verification design;
- the MBH98 retained principal-component structure;
- the MBH98 estimate of reconstruction uncertainty;
- Northern Hemisphere annual mean anomaly;
- the AD 1400–1997 period.

It therefore says that the selected years rank above the reconstructed earlier annual values under that model. It does not automatically convert “three estimated standard errors above reconstructed comparators” into a universal 99.7% probability covering unmodeled reconstruction choices or all sources of structural uncertainty. Whether that conversion is valid is for the prosecutor stages, not this map.

