Item C
Layer: assessment / reception
Provenance: RETRIEVED
Source: National Research Council (2006), Chapter 11.
Retrieved text:
“The basic conclusion of Mann et al. (1998, 1999) was that the late 20th century warmth in the Northern Hemisphere was unprecedented during at least the last 1,000 years.”

The report then says:
“Based on the analyses presented in the original papers by Mann et al. and this newer supporting evidence, the committee finds it plausible that the Northern Hemisphere was warmer during the last few decades of the 20th century than during any comparable period over the preceding millennium.”

It separately identifies:
“the original conclusions by Mann et al. (1999) that ‘the 1990s are likely the warmest decade, and 1998 the warmest year, in at least a millennium’…”

Attribution result:
- The report aggregates MBH98 and MBH99 for the broad “basic conclusion.”
- It specifically assigns the quantified millennium decade/year claim to MBH99.
- Because the assessment names the extension and its source, it does not cleanly misattribute the millennium endpoint to MBH98 alone.
Classification:
- CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED.
- The millennium claim is not recorded as MBH98-alone inflation.
- The combined label “Mann et al. (1998, 1999)” is a synthesis attribution, not proof that MBH98 independently measured the full millennium.

